WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.358
Now the other direction: the data is in your database, and a partner expects a FundsXML file.

2
00:00:06.408 --> 00:00:16.331
In this film you see the ways to generate FundsXML, the mapping behind it, how to handle several schema versions and large files, and how to test the result.

3
00:00:17.581 --> 00:00:19.990
Start with the target, not with the code.

4
00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:29.228
Take an official example file that comes close to what your partner expects, for example one of the realistic samples in the FundsXML examples repository.

5
00:00:29.278 --> 00:00:33.127
It shows which elements a complete delivery uses, in which order.

6
00:00:33.177 --> 00:00:38.226
Agree it with your partner, and you know what to build before the first line of code.

7
00:00:39.576 --> 00:00:43.677
Then the mapping: for each element, where does the value come from?

8
00:00:43.727 --> 00:00:51.175
A simple table does the job: the database column, the FundsXML path, and the rule, such as a conversion or a fixed value.

9
00:00:51.225 --> 00:00:54.707
This table is the most important document of the project.

10
00:00:54.757 --> 00:00:59.106
It is also what the business side reviews, long before any file exists.

11
00:01:00.456 --> 00:01:01.815
Which data goes to whom?

12
00:01:01.865 --> 00:01:08.857
The partner register answers it: which funds a partner receives, which kinds of data, which version and which templates.

13
00:01:08.907 --> 00:01:13.435
The generator reads the register and builds one delivery per partner and type.

14
00:01:13.485 --> 00:01:18.013
Adding a fund for a partner is then a change in the register, not in the code.

15
00:01:19.363 --> 00:01:22.306
There are three common ways to write the file.

16
00:01:22.356 --> 00:01:27.706
Templates write the X M L directly from the query results; simple and easy to read.

17
00:01:27.756 --> 00:01:33.367
X S L T transforms an intermediate X M L, for example from the database, into FundsXML.

18
00:01:33.417 --> 00:01:42.411
And code generators create classes from the schema, with JAXB in Java or xsdata in Python; the code then fills objects and writes them out.

19
00:01:43.761 --> 00:01:44.698
Which one fits?

20
00:01:44.748 --> 00:01:49.960
Templates suit small and medium deliveries and teams that like to see the X M L.

21
00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:53.447
X S L T suits setups where the data is already X M L.

22
00:01:53.497 --> 00:02:03.117
Generated code gives type safety, but the FundsXML schema is large, so the generated code is too, and it must be regenerated for every new version.

23
00:02:04.467 --> 00:02:06.704
Every file begins with ControlData.

24
00:02:06.754 --> 00:02:10.560
Generate a new document ID for every file, never reuse one.

25
00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:17.422
The generation time is now; the content date is the date the data is valid for, which is usually earlier.

26
00:02:17.472 --> 00:02:18.925
Name the data supplier.

27
00:02:18.975 --> 00:02:26.898
And set the data operation: initial for a first delivery, amend for a correction, with the ID of the delivery it corrects.

28
00:02:28.248 --> 00:02:30.900
The reference implementation uses templates.

29
00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:37.718
This one writes a share class with its prices: the identifiers, the currency, and the net asset value per date.

30
00:02:37.768 --> 00:02:41.465
The values come from the tables you saw in the earlier films.

31
00:02:41.515 --> 00:02:46.931
The order of the elements follows the schema exactly, because X S D sequences are strict.

32
00:02:47.899 --> 00:02:50.143
The order matters more than many expect.

33
00:02:50.193 --> 00:02:54.042
Here two elements of a share class were written the wrong way round.

34
00:02:54.092 --> 00:03:00.465
The schema does not accept it: the validator reports invalid content, and names the element it expected instead.

35
00:03:00.515 --> 00:03:06.315
The fix is in the generator, which must follow the sequence of the schema exactly, element by element.

36
00:03:07.465 --> 00:03:11.310
A rule that saves many errors: leave out what you do not have.

37
00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:16.398
An empty element is not the same as a missing one, and it often fails validation.

38
00:03:16.448 --> 00:03:19.225
Never write placeholders such as N A or zero.

39
00:03:19.275 --> 00:03:25.382
And if a required element has no value, the problem is in the data; fix it there, not in the file.

40
00:03:26.732 --> 00:03:29.173
Formats are the next source of trouble.

41
00:03:29.223 --> 00:03:35.689
Numbers use a decimal point and no thousands separators, whatever the language settings of the server.

42
00:03:35.739 --> 00:03:37.925
Dates are written year, month, day.

43
00:03:37.975 --> 00:03:39.650
Booleans are true or false.

44
00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:41.183
The file is U T F eight.

45
00:03:41.233 --> 00:03:44.889
And percentages are plain numbers, without a percent sign.

46
00:03:46.139 --> 00:03:49.640
Here is the generated file, opened in FreeXmlToolkit.

47
00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:52.119
Validation against the schema: valid.

48
00:03:52.169 --> 00:03:54.398
The data-quality check: no errors.

49
00:03:54.448 --> 00:04:00.898
Every generated file goes through exactly these checks before it leaves the house, automatically.

50
00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:03.821
Corrections are generated too.

51
00:04:03.871 --> 00:04:10.980
A correction is a new file with its own document ID, the data operation amend, and the ID of the delivery it corrects.

52
00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:16.744
It carries the complete corrected data for that fund and date, not just the one changed number.

53
00:04:16.794 --> 00:04:22.022
Then the receiver can simply replace what it has, as you saw in the film about loading.

54
00:04:23.372 --> 00:04:31.361
For large deliveries, write the file as a stream too: with the X M L stream writer in Java, or incremental writing in Python.

55
00:04:31.411 --> 00:04:34.962
Generate one fund at a time, so that memory stays small.

56
00:04:35.012 --> 00:04:39.206
Very large deliveries can be split, for example one file per fund.

57
00:04:39.256 --> 00:04:41.521
And compress the file for transport.

58
00:04:42.871 --> 00:04:44.954
Partners expect different versions.

59
00:04:45.004 --> 00:04:52.327
Keep one generator and describe the differences between the versions in a table, rather than copying the whole generator.

60
00:04:52.377 --> 00:04:57.506
New elements in FundsXML are usually optional, so a newer version mostly adds fields.

61
00:04:57.556 --> 00:05:00.857
Test every version you deliver, against its own schema.

62
00:05:02.207 --> 00:05:08.475
Partners have wishes: one wants the E S G template, another only some share classes, a third no documents.

63
00:05:08.525 --> 00:05:11.038
Handle these in configuration, not in code.

64
00:05:11.088 --> 00:05:16.581
The register says which blocks and templates each partner gets, and the generator follows it.

65
00:05:16.631 --> 00:05:23.316
A copy of the generator per partner is the fastest way into the trouble you saw in the first film of this series.

66
00:05:24.666 --> 00:05:32.160
Generation usually has a short time window: between the approval of the data in the evening and the cut-off time of the partners.

67
00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:34.850
Generate funds in parallel, one fund per task.

68
00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:39.411
Start each delivery as soon as its data is approved, not at a fixed late hour.

69
00:05:39.461 --> 00:05:44.440
And measure how long it takes, so that you notice early when growth makes it too slow.

70
00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:50.743
Test with golden files: a fixed set of input data and the file it must produce.

71
00:05:50.793 --> 00:05:58.469
After every change, the generator runs again, and the result is compared with the golden file, then validated and checked.

72
00:05:58.519 --> 00:06:05.055
Include the difficult cases: a correction, a fund without positions, a share class in a second currency.

73
00:06:06.405 --> 00:06:07.527
Automate the tests.

74
00:06:07.577 --> 00:06:19.669
In the reference implementation, every change runs through the same steps on GitHub: start the database, load the example files, export them again, validate the export, run the data-quality checks.

75
00:06:19.719 --> 00:06:24.045
A change that breaks the output is noticed before it reaches a partner.

76
00:06:25.395 --> 00:06:27.899
After the checks, the file is handed over.

77
00:06:27.949 --> 00:06:33.371
It gets the agreed name, is compressed if it is large, and goes out on the agreed channel.

78
00:06:33.421 --> 00:06:36.654
Where possible, the receiver confirms that it arrived.

79
00:06:36.704 --> 00:06:44.496
And the file goes into the archive, together with its validation result, so that it can always be found again by its document ID.

80
00:06:45.846 --> 00:06:48.213
Sometimes a partner asks for a file again.

81
00:06:48.263 --> 00:06:51.895
Send the archived file, unchanged and with the same document ID.

82
00:06:51.945 --> 00:07:00.581
Do not generate it again from the database, because the data may have changed since, and the partner would receive different numbers under the same ID.

83
00:07:00.631 --> 00:07:04.378
If the content has to change, that is a correction, with a new ID.

84
00:07:05.728 --> 00:07:07.570
Finally, document the generator.

85
00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:11.411
Keep the mapping table next to the code and change both together.

86
00:07:11.461 --> 00:07:16.671
Keep a short change log for your partners: what changed in the deliveries, and from when.

87
00:07:16.721 --> 00:07:19.804
And keep one example file for every kind of delivery.

88
00:07:19.854 --> 00:07:24.177
When a partner asks how a field is filled, the answer is found in minutes.

89
00:07:25.527 --> 00:07:33.349
That was generating FundsXML: the target first, a clear mapping, careful formats, several versions, and automatic tests.

90
00:07:33.399 --> 00:07:38.662
In the last film of this series, you see how to run all this in daily operations.

91
00:07:38.712 --> 00:07:39.974
Thanks for watching.
